<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandria Eschate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/329-bce">329 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexandria the Furthest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> Eschate, 'Alexandria the Furthest', was a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> (Jaxartes) at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, at or near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Intended as a frontier stronghold against the Saka nomads beyond the river, it was the north-easternmost of Alexander's foundations and later lay on routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate.jpg" alt="Alexandria Eschate" width="237" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EuthydemusI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city stood on the south bank of the Jaxartes, at the south-western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>. Cyrus the Great had founded a city in the region as his north-easternmost outpost, Cyropolis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> later settled Greek exiles from rebellious parts of its empire in this distant corner. Alexandria Eschate was probably Cyropolis renamed and re-established. Its remains lie in the tell of the old citadel of Khojand, where Soviet and Tajik excavations have found medieval, Hellenistic and Achaemenid layers below the surface, with fortifications dating to around the 4th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>To secure Sogdiana, Alexander attacked Cyropolis and a half dozen other towns in 329 BCE. His troops entered Cyropolis through a dried-up water course under the wall; Alexander was struck by a stone on the head and neck, and Craterus was wounded by an arrow. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> puts the defenders at about 15,000 fighting men and the dead of the first phase at 8,000, although he also cites Ptolemy as saying the town surrendered from the start. Veterans of Alexander's army were settled in the new city alongside the Greek exiles already there.</p><p>Surrounded by Sogdian tribes and about 300 km north of the nearest Greek settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>, the settlers built a wall of 6.0 km, which ancient authors say took about 20 days. From 250 BCE the city likely had closer contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> under the Greco-Bactrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i">Euthydemus I</a>. Scholars have speculated that the capital of Dayuan, the Great Ionians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> records, was Alexandria Eschate. Zhang Qian reached Dayuan around 130 BCE, and the Han conquered it after the War of the Heavenly Horses, which helped open the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. According to Curtius, the city kept its Greek culture as late as 30 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site lies within the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, where the oldest surface remains of the walls date only to the 10th century. Household utensils, armaments and building materials from the excavations are exhibited in the Museum of Regional Studies in Khojand, and the site has also yielded numerous Hellenistic coins and pottery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexandria Eschate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other cities include <b>Alexandria Eschate</b>, Eucratideia and an Amphipolis named after the Macedonian city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
